<article class="env-proposition" id="07PJ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07PJ">Proposition <span data-tag="07PJ">15.49.7</span>.</a> The following types of rings are J-2: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
fields, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Noetherian complete local rings, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\mathbf{Z}$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Noetherian local rings of dimension $1$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Nagata rings of dimension $1$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Dedekind domains with fraction field of characteristic zero, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
finite type ring extensions of any of the above. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      For cases (1), (3), (5), and (6) this is proved by checking condition (4) of Lemma <a href="/tag/07PC" data-tag="07PC">15.48.6</a>. We will only do this in case $R$ is a Nagata ring of dimension $1$. Let $\mathfrak p \subset R$ be a prime ideal and let $L/\kappa (\mathfrak p)$ be a finite purely inseparable extension. If $\mathfrak p \subset R$ is a maximal ideal, then $R \to L$ is finite and $L$ is a regular ring and we've checked the condition. If $\mathfrak p \subset R$ is a minimal prime, then the Nagata condition insures that the integral closure $R' \subset L$ of $R$ in $L$ is finite over $R$. Then $R'$ is a normal domain of dimension $1$ (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00OJ" data-tag="00OJ">10.112.3</a>) hence regular (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/031S" data-tag="031S">10.157.4</a>) and we've checked the condition in this case as well. 
    <p>
      For case (2), we will use condition (3) of Lemma <a href="/tag/07PC" data-tag="07PC">15.48.6</a>. Let $R$ be a Noetherian complete local ring. Note that if $R \to R'$ is finite, then $R'$ is a product of Noetherian complete local rings, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0325" data-tag="0325">10.160.2</a>. Thus by Lemma <a href="/tag/07P9" data-tag="07P9">15.48.3</a> it suffices to prove that a Noetherian complete local ring which is a domain is J-0, which is Lemma <a href="/tag/07PI" data-tag="07PI">15.49.6</a>. 
    <p>
      For case (4), we also use condition (3) of Lemma <a href="/tag/07PC" data-tag="07PC">15.48.6</a>. Namely, if $R$ is a local Noetherian ring of dimension $1$ and $R \to R'$ is finite, then $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R')$ is finite. Since the regular locus is stable under generalization, we see that $R'$ is J-1. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>